comments.body:
@participant24 this has no impact on images that are already loaded. Though it is possible that backgrounding your application is causing the images to get disposed, and returning to the foreground is forcing them to re-load.

I can test more with https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/164374 . How many cycles is sufficient to trigger the failure?

You can also test on master as well if you have an easier time reproducing it.

@participant16 
From my test this time, I can feel that the effect has greatly improved, so much so that my current program has changed from a must occurrence to an occasional occurrence.
If we design the sample code based on the current version, the problem is that a large number of images need to be filled in, which undoubtedly increases the feedback cost. But I will be in the near future to create a simple app to push the issue.

> > To recap, if I’m not mistaken, this issue occurs in 3.29, but does not occur in 3.27 when Impeller is disabled (although this is not a viable option due to the Skia issues on iOS). Currently, the issue is unassigned after the first fix attempt failed. 3.24 remains the only viable solution to ship the apps, although there’s a flickering introduced in 3.20, which might be fixed in 3.27, but it’s not certain, as the flickering issues were closed by the GitHub bot and were not specifically addressed in any PR. Some are still using 3.19 to avoid above situation, tough this version had its issues too. Potentially, both the flickering and the unloaded images issues may still exist on master.
> 
> My project uses `webview/flutter_inappwebview`, and in Flutter version 3.24, another issue ([#158961](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/158961)) occurs, so it must be upgraded to Flutter 3.27.

@participant8 can you please let me know if you experience this issue with 3.19.6? If you don't, we will also downgrade to this version. 

@participant30, thanks for sharing this! We were not aware of that other issue. Since you are forced on 3.27, have you discovered any reasonable workarounds to prevent images from being unloaded when app is resumed from background?

We have a record of the latest stable versions and the issues that are preventing adoption in production. It is becoming increasingly challenging to upgrade and keep up with the ongoing issues.

> If you are not seeing the error message "Image upload failed due to loss of GPU access." it's a different issue for sure that needs reporting.

@participant27 is there a space (like Discussions or Discord) where we can engage with the Flutter team about improving the bug reporting process? Honestly, the current flow doesn’t work well - just look at what’s happened since 3.7, with stable releases becoming practically unusable in production. The community can help, but we can’t effectively report those many kind of issues that your QA team should already be aware of. 

If community involvement is more necessary than usual, maybe we could consider inviting contributors to stress-test the latest beta 3–4 weeks before promotion to stable. Othewise, as a startup, we simply don’t have the time or resources to track this many issues on stable. Despite our best intentions, it’s just not feasible.

@participant16 if we play table tennis and I served to you 64 ping pong balls, could you return them?  If you said "it depends on how you serve them", you are correct.  Just looking at the number 64 isn't enough to make an informed decision about wether it is adequate or not.

We can't make the prioritizations based on emotions, it should be able to be demonstrated empirically.  It may be that in attempting to demonstrate something empirically, it may lead us to a wholly different bug, great.  Having strong emotions about a number isn't going to help anyone, that energy could instead be spent on proving me wrong or discovering a different bug that you are actually running into.

🙏
